1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,940
The whole subject of Yom Tov Sheni when

2
00:00:01,940 --> 00:00:03,600
you have somebody from Eretz Yisroel who comes

3
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,080
to the United States or somebody from America

4
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:07,900
who goes to Eretz Yisroel and the question

5
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:10,119
of what to do in both directions. It’s

6
00:00:10,119 --> 00:00:12,259
a different question in both directions but there

7
00:00:12,259 --> 00:00:14,179
are a number of important issues that we

8
00:00:14,179 --> 00:00:16,539
need to think about. Number one, when I

9
00:00:16,539 --> 00:00:18,179
go to the opposite, I don’t know what

10
00:00:18,179 --> 00:00:19,699
you call it, the opposite gender, when I

11
00:00:19,699 --> 00:00:22,839
go to the opposite location am I supposed

12
00:00:22,839 --> 00:00:24,980
to behave the way they do or am

13
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:27,219
I supposed to do what I originally was

14
00:00:27,219 --> 00:00:29,980
supposed to do from my origin, from the

15
00:00:29,980 --> 00:00:31,820
location where I actually live? That’s question number

16
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:33,240
one we have to talk about. Question number

17
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:35,719
two is let’s say I’m an Eretz Yisroel

18
00:00:35,719 --> 00:00:37,119
and I’m keeping two days of Yom Tov

19
00:00:37,179 --> 00:00:38,320
which we’re going to have to discuss in

20
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:40,460
a moment, if that’s what I’m doing, would

21
00:00:40,460 --> 00:00:41,799
I be allowed to go to someone who

22
00:00:41,799 --> 00:00:44,320
is an Israeli, a legitimate Israeli and say

23
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:46,840
to them, look, I’m keeping Yom Tov today,

24
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:48,579
for me it’s Yom Tov Sheni, would you

25
00:00:48,579 --> 00:00:49,719
be able to turn the lights on for

26
00:00:49,719 --> 00:00:51,619
me? Am I allowed to instruct somebody who

27
00:00:51,619 --> 00:00:53,439
is not observing Yom Tov to do the

28
00:00:53,439 --> 00:00:55,240
Melacha for me? Question number three, let’s say

29
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:57,719
I go to Eretz Yisroel and I am

30
00:00:57,719 --> 00:00:59,140
of the opinion that I should only keep

31
00:00:59,140 --> 00:01:01,179
one day of Yom Tov, there are legitimate

32
00:01:01,179 --> 00:01:02,979
opinions who say that, there are many people

33
00:01:02,979 --> 00:01:05,000
who do that, big Machlokes as we’ll see

34
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,680
in a few minutes, let’s say that is

35
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:08,180
what I’m doing, would I be allowed to

36
00:01:08,180 --> 00:01:10,446
fly home back to America on Yom Tov

37
00:01:10,446 --> 00:01:12,280
Sheni? I don’t want to miss another day

38
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:13,400
of work, I don’t want to have to

39
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:14,859
wait until after Yom Tov is over and

40
00:01:14,859 --> 00:01:17,780
then fly back after Yom Tov and miss

41
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:19,620
a whole day or potentially two days because

42
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:21,420
maybe it’s Shabbos right after, whatever it’s going

43
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:23,459
to be, it will certainly make my life

44
00:01:23,459 --> 00:01:25,239
easier if I fly home on Yom Tov

45
00:01:25,239 --> 00:01:26,620
Sheni. Would I be allowed to do that

46
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:29,019
and if so is there a way that

47
00:01:29,019 --> 00:01:30,359
I’m supposed to do that? Those are the

48
00:01:30,359 --> 00:01:32,180
questions that I would like to discuss. We

49
00:01:32,180 --> 00:01:33,939
don’t have that much time so maybe you’ll

50
00:01:33,939 --> 00:01:36,379
hold off on your question and we’ll get

51
00:01:36,379 --> 00:01:38,219
it later. We begin with a Mishna in

52
00:01:38,219 --> 00:01:40,560
Masechet Psachim on Daf Nun where the Mishna

53
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:42,680
goes through a whole list of different Minhagim

54
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:45,000
that certain communities have and other communities do

55
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:46,879
not have. And the Mishna says, well what

56
00:01:46,879 --> 00:01:48,780
happens when you have a clash? What happens

57
00:01:48,780 --> 00:01:50,659
when I have a community where I grew

58
00:01:50,659 --> 00:01:52,739
up that has this Minhag and now I

59
00:01:52,739 --> 00:01:55,099
get married or I move locations and I’m

60
00:01:55,099 --> 00:01:57,359
now finding myself in a new place which

61
00:01:57,359 --> 00:01:58,879
has very different Minhagim? So what do I

62
00:01:58,879 --> 00:02:00,920
do then? The Gemara says I should always

63
00:02:00,920 --> 00:02:03,379
do Ke-minhag Ha-makom, I should always do like

64
00:02:03,379 --> 00:02:05,235
the Minhag of the location where I live

65
00:02:05,235 --> 00:02:08,460
V'al yeshane adam mipnei ha-machlokes, I should not

66
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:10,400
do differently than other people in the neighborhood

67
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:13,300
because it will cause dispute, it will cause

68
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:15,639
distance between myself and the rest of the

69
00:02:15,639 --> 00:02:17,599
community and therefore the Gemara advises that we

70
00:02:17,599 --> 00:02:20,079
should always make sure to follow the Minhag

71
00:02:20,079 --> 00:02:22,699
Ha-makom. Now, how far does that go? It’s

72
00:02:22,699 --> 00:02:24,620
a good question. Let’s say I move to

73
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:26,800
a community where everybody’s from German descent and

74
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:28,620
they only wait three hours but my family

75
00:02:28,620 --> 00:02:30,986
waits six hours, that’s not called being Meshane

76
00:02:30,986 --> 00:02:33,759
miminhag hamakom, that is my personal custom that

77
00:02:33,759 --> 00:02:35,180
happens in my house, no one’s going to

78
00:02:35,180 --> 00:02:37,460
be uncomfortable about the fact that I’m doing

79
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:38,840
that. Or if I have a Chumra on

80
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:40,560
Shabbos that I choose not to use the

81
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:43,580
Eiruv, that’s not called being Meshane mipnei ha-machlokes,

82
00:02:43,639 --> 00:02:45,099
everybody uses the Eiruv, I have a right

83
00:02:45,099 --> 00:02:47,199
to be Machmir. What the Gemara here is

84
00:02:47,199 --> 00:02:48,780
talking about is let’s say if you have

85
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:51,439
whatever, it doesn’t matter, but the Gemara basically

86
00:02:51,439 --> 00:02:53,259
says the rule is whenever I have a

87
00:02:53,259 --> 00:02:55,740
certain custom in a community, I should follow

88
00:02:55,740 --> 00:02:58,340
that custom. For example, writes the Gemara, let’s

89
00:02:58,340 --> 00:03:00,419
say I’m in a community where they’re keeping

90
00:03:00,419 --> 00:03:03,900
two days of Yom Tov and I happen

91
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:05,319
to come from a place where they only

92
00:03:05,319 --> 00:03:07,280
keep one day of Yom Tov, so for

93
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:09,159
example I’m from Eretz Yisroel and now I

94
00:03:09,159 --> 00:03:11,080
come to the United States where they all

95
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:12,400
keep two days of Yom Tov and for

96
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:14,379
whatever reason I happen to be here instead

97
00:03:14,379 --> 00:03:16,419
of being at home in Eretz Yisroel, says

98
00:03:16,419 --> 00:03:17,819
the Gemara, I have to make sure that

99
00:03:17,819 --> 00:03:20,439
I don’t do anything on the second day

100
00:03:20,439 --> 00:03:22,419
of Yom Tov because I’m in a community

101
00:03:22,419 --> 00:03:23,939
where no one’s doing anything on the second

102
00:03:23,939 --> 00:03:24,879
day of Yom Tov. What’s it going to

103
00:03:24,879 --> 00:03:27,879
look like when nobody’s driving, nobody’s handling their

104
00:03:27,879 --> 00:03:29,900
phones, nobody’s on their computer, and I’m walking

105
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:31,500
up and down the streets driving my car

106
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:33,560
with my phone out, I have my computer,

107
00:03:33,699 --> 00:03:35,539
I have my iPad, what would that look

108
00:03:35,539 --> 00:03:36,860
like? Says the Gemara, that would be very

109
00:03:36,860 --> 00:03:38,479
uncomfortable and therefore you would not be allowed

110
00:03:38,479 --> 00:03:40,419
to do it. With one caveat, if you’re

111
00:03:40,419 --> 00:03:42,020
from Eretz Yisroel and now you happen to

112
00:03:42,020 --> 00:03:44,310
be in Chutz L'aretz, if you are Betoch

113
00:03:44,310 --> 00:03:47,060
Ha-yishuv, if you’re within the confines of the

114
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:49,039
Jewish community then you are restricted, you are

115
00:03:49,039 --> 00:03:51,939
limited to do what the Jewish community does.

116
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:55,699
However, if you are Ba-midbar, let’s say I

117
00:03:55,699 --> 00:03:58,020
come to the United States and I happen

118
00:03:58,020 --> 00:04:00,460
to be in a hospital, I have a

119
00:04:00,460 --> 00:04:02,020
family member in a hospital somewhere and there

120
00:04:02,020 --> 00:04:04,379
is no Jewish community anywhere around me and

121
00:04:04,379 --> 00:04:06,439
I’m really a Ben Eretz Yisroel and for

122
00:04:06,439 --> 00:04:08,240
Yom Tov I’m not going to be anywhere

123
00:04:08,240 --> 00:04:11,240
within the Tchum of a Jewish community, in

124
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:13,000
that case says the Gemara I’m allowed to

125
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:16,139
observe one day of Yom Tov. Why? Because

126
00:04:16,139 --> 00:04:18,800
I’m not being Meshane miminhag hamakom, there is

127
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:21,360
no Makom, there is no Minhag here, I’m

128
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:23,079
not part of the community, I’m not together

129
00:04:23,079 --> 00:04:25,040
with anybody else and therefore there would be

130
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:27,259
no problem for me to do Melacha in

131
00:04:27,259 --> 00:04:30,360
that circumstance. Writes the Shulchan Aruch, this is

132
00:04:30,720 --> 00:04:32,633
paskened in Shulchan Aruch Bnei Eretz Yisroel shebau

133
00:04:32,633 --> 00:04:35,069
l'chutz l'aretz, if you have Jews from Eretz

134
00:04:35,069 --> 00:04:36,879
Yisroel who normally only keep one day of

135
00:04:36,879 --> 00:04:38,080
Yom Tov and now they come to Chutz

136
00:04:38,080 --> 00:04:40,160
L'aretz where we keep two days, says the

137
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:41,639
Gemara, they are not allowed to do Melacha

138
00:04:41,639 --> 00:04:44,720
on Yom Tov Sheni if they find themselves

139
00:04:44,720 --> 00:04:47,259
within the confines of the Jewish community on

140
00:04:47,259 --> 00:04:49,939
Yom Tov Sheni. However, what the Shulchan Aruch

141
00:04:49,939 --> 00:04:52,939
here does not say is what kind of

142
00:04:52,939 --> 00:04:55,850
Melacha are they not allowed to do. Shulchan

143
00:04:55,850 --> 00:04:57,480
Aruch just says they can’t do Melacha. What

144
00:04:57,480 --> 00:05:02,500
about Melacha b'tzina? Which means once nobody else

145
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:04,420
is around and I'm in the privacy of

146
00:05:04,420 --> 00:05:06,899
my own home. So it's true I'm in

147
00:05:06,899 --> 00:05:08,800
a Jewish community here in chutz l'aretz but

148
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:10,879
I'm in my own room. Nobody's looking into

149
00:05:10,879 --> 00:05:12,480
the windows of my room. Would I be

150
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:14,040
allowed to use my phone or my computer

151
00:05:14,040 --> 00:05:16,040
in my room or would you say no?

152
00:05:16,339 --> 00:05:17,759
Once I'm in chutz l'aretz I have to

153
00:05:17,759 --> 00:05:20,420
observe Yom Tov Sheni like everybody else and

154
00:05:20,420 --> 00:05:22,459
I'm not allowed to distinguish myself from them

155
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:24,720
even if it is b'tzina, even if it's

156
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:26,860
in the privacy of my own home where

157
00:05:26,860 --> 00:05:28,819
nobody else is going to be. That is

158
00:05:28,819 --> 00:05:31,879
a major dispute among the Rishonim. Tosafot famously

159
00:05:31,879 --> 00:05:34,399
writes in Masechet Pesachim that even devarim sheb'tzina

160
00:05:34,399 --> 00:05:36,899
would not be permissible because once you come

161
00:05:36,899 --> 00:05:38,680
to a place where they keep two days

162
00:05:38,680 --> 00:05:40,519
of Yom Tov, you have to observe two

163
00:05:40,519 --> 00:05:41,839
days of Yom Tov just like them and

164
00:05:41,839 --> 00:05:43,060
you would not be allowed to do melacha.

165
00:05:43,399 --> 00:05:45,379
There are a number of great Rishonim who

166
00:05:45,379 --> 00:05:48,639
disagree. Great Rishonim means the Ran, the Rosh,

167
00:05:48,839 --> 00:05:51,360
the Rambam. Those are major major Rishonim who

168
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:54,259
disagree. And very surprisingly even though the Rambam

169
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:56,420
and the Rosh and the Ran all disagree

170
00:05:56,420 --> 00:05:58,600
with the Ba'alei haTosafot, the Shulchan Aruch I'm

171
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:01,160
sorry the Mishnah Berurah and the Magen Avraham,

172
00:06:01,220 --> 00:06:04,439
all the classical poskim give their unequivocal opinion

173
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:06,740
which is that you are not allowed to

174
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:09,600
do melacha even b'tzina when you are in

175
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:11,593
chutz l'aretz even if you're a Ben Eretz

176
00:06:11,593 --> 00:06:13,939
Yisrael, even if you're behind closed doors, even

177
00:06:13,939 --> 00:06:15,879
if you think nobody's watching, it would not

178
00:06:15,879 --> 00:06:18,373
be something that would be permissible. Rav Moshe

179
00:06:18,373 --> 00:06:19,839
Feinstein has a tshuvah: are you allowed to

180
00:06:19,839 --> 00:06:21,939
take a shower, a hot shower on Yom

181
00:06:21,939 --> 00:06:23,513
Tov Sheni if you are a Ben Eretz

182
00:06:23,513 --> 00:06:25,040
Yisrael who happens to be in America? So

183
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:26,699
Rav Moshe says probably in terms of the

184
00:06:26,699 --> 00:06:29,060
shower it would be permissible. Why? Because there

185
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:31,240
are permissible ways for somebody who's observing Yom

186
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:34,100
Tov to take a shower himself. So nobody

187
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:36,139
automatically knows that you're doing anything wrong. When

188
00:06:36,139 --> 00:06:37,360
you go in the shower maybe you're doing

189
00:06:37,360 --> 00:06:38,519
it the right way. Maybe you're taking a

190
00:06:38,519 --> 00:06:40,060
cold shower. Maybe you're doing what the Gemara

191
00:06:40,060 --> 00:06:42,660
says eiver eiver which means you're only bathing

192
00:06:42,660 --> 00:06:44,720
one part of your body at a time,

193
00:06:44,759 --> 00:06:46,759
which is permissible on Yom Tov. So the

194
00:06:46,759 --> 00:06:49,699
Gemara I'm sorry Rav Moshe Feinstein says there

195
00:06:49,699 --> 00:06:51,220
are certain things that you may be allowed

196
00:06:51,220 --> 00:06:53,620
to do but in general he says this

197
00:06:53,620 --> 00:06:56,000
would be something that we should certainly be

198
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:57,740
careful about. The Mishnah Berurah as I said

199
00:06:57,879 --> 00:06:59,620
is machmir and he says like the Ba'alei

200
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:01,579
haTosafot that when the Shulchan Aruch says you're

201
00:07:01,579 --> 00:07:03,139
not allowed to do melacha in chutz l'aretz

202
00:07:03,259 --> 00:07:05,800
it means even b'tzina, even in the privacy

203
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:07,680
of your own home it would not be

204
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:10,139
permissible. Now I found a very interesting comment

205
00:07:10,139 --> 00:07:13,040
last night actually of Rav Nevenzahl in his

206
00:07:13,040 --> 00:07:14,480
comments on the Mishnah Berurah where he says

207
00:07:14,540 --> 00:07:17,199
yes maybe that was back then when it

208
00:07:17,199 --> 00:07:19,360
was so unusual for people to travel to

209
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:21,379
different communities and if you were going to

210
00:07:21,379 --> 00:07:23,480
do something different than everyone else people would

211
00:07:23,480 --> 00:07:26,139
say wow why is this person acting differently?

212
00:07:26,339 --> 00:07:27,680
Why is he sticking out? Why isn't he

213
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:30,180
following the protocol? But now says Rav Nevenzahl

214
00:07:30,259 --> 00:07:32,319
everybody knows there are always people flying back

215
00:07:32,319 --> 00:07:34,100
and forth from America to Eretz Yisrael and

216
00:07:34,100 --> 00:07:37,439
vice versa and it wouldn't even make you

217
00:07:37,439 --> 00:07:39,600
feel uncomfortable if you saw somebody doing melacha

218
00:07:39,600 --> 00:07:40,920
here on Yom Tov Sheni. What would you

219
00:07:40,920 --> 00:07:44,420
say? Your conclusion would be not that this

220
00:07:44,420 --> 00:07:46,259
person is not a religious person but rather

221
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:48,300
this person must be from Israel even if

222
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:51,060
I don't know them. Right? You see somebody

223
00:07:51,060 --> 00:07:53,040
walking down the street with a yarmulke and

224
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:54,660
tzitzit on Yom Tov Sheni on their phone

225
00:07:55,300 --> 00:07:56,839
you would understand either the person is a

226
00:07:56,839 --> 00:07:59,120
doctor and therefore it's pikuach nefesh or you

227
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:01,620
would say the person is from Eretz Yisrael

228
00:08:01,620 --> 00:08:04,199
and they're visiting here. And nobody says Rav

229
00:08:04,199 --> 00:08:06,060
Nevenzahl he thinks that it's a very different

230
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:09,139
ballgame today when we deal with the situation

231
00:08:09,139 --> 00:08:10,879
that we're in where people are traveling all

232
00:08:10,879 --> 00:08:13,120
the time. He suggests that maybe this would

233
00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:15,560
not be a problem. Again that's a tremendous

234
00:08:15,560 --> 00:08:17,560
chiddush. I don't know if that is accepted.

235
00:08:17,560 --> 00:08:19,579
My father definitely does not agree with that.

236
00:08:19,720 --> 00:08:21,000
He says we should follow the psak of

237
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,199
the Mishnah Berurah which is that even today

238
00:08:23,300 --> 00:08:25,139
when we have people traveling back and forth

239
00:08:25,180 --> 00:08:27,019
we have to be careful if you find

240
00:08:27,019 --> 00:08:29,000
yourself a Ben Eretz Yisrael who finds themselves

241
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:30,540
in chutz l'aretz is not allowed to do

242
00:08:30,540 --> 00:08:34,600
melacha on Yom Tov Sheni here even b'tzina.

243
00:08:34,700 --> 00:08:36,000
And my father likes to tell the story

244
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,279
that there was once an individual who came,

245
00:08:38,340 --> 00:08:39,680
he was a big talmid chacham from Eretz

246
00:08:39,680 --> 00:08:41,299
